---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHeader from '../../components/PageHeader.astro';
import headerBg from '../../assets/blog/boots/badlands-dangerous-cliff.jpg';
import notchLadder from '../../assets/blog/boots/badlands-notch-ladder.jpg';
import keenBoots from '../../assets/blog/boots/keen-boots.jpg';
import dangerousCliff from '../../assets/blog/boots/badlands-dangerous-cliff.jpg';
---

<Base
  title="A Pair of Hiking Boots"
  description="A worn-out pair of boots, a 2017 road trip to the Badlands, and the people who were there. By Dr. Mohsen Tahmasebi Nasab."
>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="// blog"
    title="A Pair of Hiking Boots"
    lede="A sole coming apart in a river in Puerto Rico, and everything the boots had walked through since 2017."
    bgImage={headerBg}
    bgPos="center 55%"
  >
    <p class="post-meta">Sep 2, 2026 · about a 2 minute read</p>
  </PageHeader>

  <article class="section">
    <div class="wrap post">
      <p>
        I've never thought of myself as someone who gets attached to things. Or maybe it is more
        accurate to say that I didn't think I attached sentimental value to objects. At least not
        until recently.
      </p>

      <p>
        A week ago, I was standing in the Río Yauco, upstream of Lago Lucchetti in Puerto Rico,
        when I noticed that the sole of one of my hiking boots was separating from the rest of the
        shoe. Normally, that would have been it. The boots were worn out. I would throw them away,
        buy another pair, and move on.
      </p>

      <figure class="post-fig">
        <Image
          src={keenBoots}
          alt="A worn pair of Keen hiking boots with teal and tan panels, laces loose, sitting on a blue carpet by a window"
          widths={[480, 800, 1200]}
          sizes="(min-width: 800px) 720px, 100vw"
        />
        <figcaption>The boots, nine years in.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        But standing there in the river, I realized these boots meant more to me than I had
        expected. I bought them in 2017, when I was getting ready for one of my first real
        adventures in the U.S., a road trip to the Badlands in South Dakota. I wasn't going alone.
        Hassan and Amin had signed up for the trip too.
      </p>

      <figure class="post-fig">
        <Image
          src={notchLadder}
          alt="Looking down a wooden ladder bolted to a steep white rock face in the Badlands, with a hiker partway down"
          widths={[480, 800, 1200]}
          sizes="(min-width: 800px) 720px, 100vw"
        />
        <figcaption>Badlands National Park, South Dakota, 2017.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        I remember thinking surprisingly hard about whether I should buy those boots. They cost
        around $100. I had no idea what I was actually buying.
      </p>

      <figure class="post-fig">
        <Image
          src={dangerousCliff}
          alt="A hiker walking along a ridge in the Badlands at dusk, with a sign in the foreground reading Dangerous Cliff, Keep Right"
          widths={[480, 800, 1200]}
          sizes="(min-width: 800px) 720px, 100vw"
        />
        <figcaption>Keep right.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Looking back now, I feel grateful for the places I have had the chance to see. But even
        more than that, I'm grateful for the people who have been part of those adventures. The
        friends who were there at the beginning. The people I met along the way. The conversations,
        wrong turns, long drives, exhausting hikes, and small moments that I probably did not
        realize at the time would stay with me.
      </p>

      <p>
        I still don't think I'm someone who gets particularly attached to objects. But maybe, every
        once in a while, an object stays with you long enough that it stops being just an object.
      </p>

      <p>
        Sometimes it becomes a way of remembering who you were when you first picked it up,
        everywhere you went afterward, and how much life happened in between.
      </p>

      <p class="post-signoff">Mohsen :)</p>

      <p class="post-back"><a href="/blog">← Back to blog</a></p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .post-meta {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    font: 600 0.82rem/1 var(--font-sans);
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-faint);
  }

  .post { max-width: 720px; }

  .post > p {
    color: var(--text-dim);
    line-height: 1.75;
    margin: 0 0 1.15rem;
    font-size: 1.03rem;
  }
  .post > p a { color: var(--accent); }
  .post > p a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }

  .post-fig {
    margin: 1.9rem 0;
  }
  .post-fig :global(img) {
    width: 100%; height: auto; display: block;
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-soft);
  }
  .post-fig figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.7rem;
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    color: var(--text-faint);
    line-height: 1.55;
    text-align: center;
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .post-signoff {
    margin-top: 2rem;
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--text-dim);
  }
  .post-back {
    margin-top: 2.4rem;
    padding-top: 1.4rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border-soft);
    font: 600 0.85rem/1 var(--font-sans);
  }
  .post-back a { color: var(--text-faint); }
  .post-back a:hover { color: var(--accent); }
</style>
